    feat(jev): wire the JEV tier into the overworld decision loop. Addresses DF-JEV-1.
    
    The overworld cycle was still decided by controller_plan(), and plan_entry read
    jev_answered/escalated/missing_class off a controller payload that never sets
    those keys — so every real run logged autonomy 0/16 even though JEV-1/2/4 were
    complete. JEV was reachable only from the starter-menu and battle-recovery
    branches; PRD v3 stages 5-6 were proven by scripts/jev_projection_probe.py but
    never consumed by the game loop.
    
    cron_runner.py:
    
    - _jev_overworld_decision(obs, ...) — the new seam. It builds the bounded state
      projection through the same state_projection.build() call, with the same
      cross-cycle material (goal, per-tile repeat counts, recent events, last
      action, mechanics rules) and the same last_action_failed value as
      scripts/jev_projection_probe.py, then asks jev_client.decide. Fail closed:
      the returned dict is EMPTY whenever JEV errored or next_action is outside the
      overworld button vocabulary, and WAIT maps to an EMPTY plan — an unreadable
      answer can never become an invented press. A raising tier is caught, printed
      and treated as a miss, so a broken tier cannot hide behind an autonomy zero.
    - main() overworld branch: JEV is consulted before the controller. A hit sets
      the plan, stamps pipeline="jev" and skips the controller call and its image
      tokens; a miss falls through to controller_plan() exactly as before.
    - Per-tile repeat counts for the CURRENT map are tracked (reset on map change)
      as the projection's stuck signal, and a direction press that left the player
      on the same tile is handed to the gate as the failure trigger (PRD v3 §3.2
      trigger 1).
    - plan_entry stamps pipeline from the pipeline that actually decided,
      raw_distribution from JEV's real payload (the acceptance field) and
      jev_projection_chars; the stale comment claiming those keys are never set is
      gone. The per-cycle log line names the deciding pipeline.
    
    Verified (no live LLM cycles were run — that is the foreman's job):
    
    - .venv/bin/python -m pytest -m "not heavy" -q → 4023 passed, 8 skipped
    - .venv/bin/mypy src/ --ignore-missing-imports → no issues in 66 source files
    - .venv/bin/ruff check . → all checks passed
    - python3 cron_runner.py --dry-run → exit 0
    - RED proof: re-wiring the row back to pipeline_name / raw_distribution None
      fails TestPlanEntryRowShape, so the row-shape guards are not vacuous.
    
    tests/test_cron_runner_metrics.py also carries ~20 formatter-only rewraps of
    pre-existing lines: the file was already ruff-format-dirty at HEAD (verified on
    the HEAD blob), and the Tier-1 lint lane checks the whole file, so the gate
    cannot pass while it stays dirty. No behaviour changes in those lines.
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
